# Medieval and Early Modern Philosophy: Faith, Reason, and the Rise of Science

> Medieval thinkers reasoned about God with Greek tools; the early
> moderns rebuilt philosophy on the knowing subject, split into
> rationalists and empiricists, and political philosophy followed.

## 1. Augustine and Aquinas: Faith Seeking Understanding

**Augustine** (354–430 CE) baptised Plato: the Forms become ideas in
God's mind, and the restless soul knows truth by divine illumination.
His *Confessions* invents the inner autobiography, and his meditation
on time remains definitive — "What then is time? If no one asks me,
I know; if I wish to explain it, I do not know." The *City of God*
frames history as a struggle between two loves.

**Thomas Aquinas** (1225–1274) baptised Aristotle. In the *Summa
Theologiae*, faith and reason form one harmony: truths of reason and
truths of revelation cannot contradict, since both derive from God.
His **five ways (quinque viae)** argue from motion, efficient causes,
contingency, degrees of perfection, and design to a first unmoved
source.

## 2. The Problem of Universals

Do general terms name real things?

| Position | Claim | Champion |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Realism | Universals exist really | Augustinians, following Plato |
| Conceptualism | Universals are concepts in minds | Peter Abelard's middle way |
| Nominalism | Only particulars exist; universals are names | William of Ockham |

[definition]
**Ockham's razor** (after William of Ockham, c. 1287–1347): entities
should not be multiplied beyond necessity — among explanations that
fit the facts equally well, prefer the one positing fewest entities.
It remains a standing rule of scientific method.
[/definition]

## 3. The Rationalists

Reason, the rationalists held, can know reality through clear ideas
independent of experience — mathematics as the model of knowledge:

| Thinker | Dates | Core doctrine | Key text |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Descartes | 1596–1650 | Cogito ergo sum; mind–body dualism | Meditations (1641) |
| Spinoza | 1632–1677 | One substance: deus sive natura | Ethics, in geometrical order |
| Leibniz | 1646–1716 | Monads; best of all possible worlds | Monadology |

**Descartes** practised **methodic doubt**: doubt everything
dubitable — senses, body, world — until one certainty survives:
doubting is thinking, so a thinker exists. *Cogito ergo sum.* Mind and
body become two distinct substances: **dualism**.

**Spinoza** radicalises him: only one substance exists, infinite —
call it God or Nature (**deus sive natura**); mind and body are two of
its attributes, and everything follows necessarily.

**Leibniz** multiplies: reality consists of countless windowless
**monads**, simple substances pre-programmed in **pre-established
harmony**; by the **principle of sufficient reason**, God chose the
best of all possible worlds.

## 4. The Empiricists

The empiricist counter-thesis: nothing in the intellect that was not
first in the senses:

| Thinker | Dates | Core doctrine | Key text |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Locke | 1632–1704 | Mind a tabula rasa; no innate ideas | Essay (1689) |
| Berkeley | 1685–1753 | Esse est percipi — to be is to be perceived | Principles |
| Hume | 1711–1776 | Skepticism about self, cause, induction | Treatise (1739) |

**Locke's** **tabula rasa** (blank slate) makes every idea a construct
of sensation and reflection. **Berkeley** drives empiricism to
idealism: we perceive only ideas, never "matter" — so **to be is to
be perceived**, and the world's steadiness is God's perceiving.

**Hume** applies the method ruthlessly and gets three results:

1. The **self dissolves** — introspection finds a **bundle** of
   perceptions, no owner.
2. **Causation dissolves** — we observe constant conjunction, never a
   necessary link; the "connection" is habit.
3. **Induction** cannot be justified without a circle.

[important title="The is–ought gap"]
Hume noticed moralists sliding from "is" statements to "ought"
conclusions without argument. No set of facts, however complete,
entails a value without an added principle — the modern starting
point of metaethics.
[/important]

## 5. The Deadlock

Rationalism could not say how the mind's clear ideas latch onto the
world; Hume dissolved necessity, self, and induction — the framework of
science itself. **Kant** will answer that the mind contributes space,
time, and causality as the form of experience: Hume, he said,
"interrupted my dogmatic slumber".

@diagram tree id="philosophy-earlymodern-split" layout="tree" aria="Early modern philosophy splits into rationalists and empiricists, answered by Kant"
  "Early modern" -> Rationalism
  "Early modern" -> Empiricism
  "Early modern" -> "Kant's synthesis"
  Rationalism -> Descartes
  Rationalism -> Spinoza
  Rationalism -> Leibniz
  Empiricism -> Locke
  Empiricism -> Berkeley
  Empiricism -> Hume
@/diagram

## 6. Political Philosophy Spins Off

| Thinker | Text | State of nature | Resulting politics |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Hobbes (1588–1679) | Leviathan (1651) | War of all against all | Absolute sovereign by contract, from fear |
| Locke | Two Treatises (1689) | Free and equal under natural law | Limited government, consent, revolution |
| Rousseau (1712–1778) | The Social Contract (1762) | Free, but corrupted by property | Rule by the general will |

**Hobbes'** state of nature makes life "solitary, poor, nasty, brutish,
and short" — the **social contract** buys peace at the price of
absolute authority. **Locke** answers with natural rights to life,
liberty, and property; **Rousseau** opens with a scandal: "Man is born
free, and everywhere he is in chains."

[summary]
Medieval philosophy joined Greek argument to religious commitment —
Augustine's Platonism, Aquinas' harmony of faith and reason, and the
realist–nominalist dispute over universals. The early moderns then
split: rationalists (Descartes, Spinoza, Leibniz) trusted reason
alone; empiricists (Locke, Berkeley, Hume) reduced knowledge to
experience and paid in skepticism. Hume set the problem Kant would
solve, while Hobbes, Locke, and Rousseau rebuilt politics on the
social contract.
[/summary]
